1. what is an Exception?
   An exception is an unwanted or unexpected event that occurs during program execution and disrupts the normal flow of
   execution.
   It is highly recommended to handle the exception and the main object of exception handling is graceful termination of
   program.

2. what is Compiler?
   “A compiler is a program that translates source code into bytecode or machine code. In Java, the Java compiler (javac)
   converts the .java source file into a .class file containing bytecode.

3. what is JVM?
   “JVM (Java Virtual Machine) is an abstract machine that executes Java bytecode present in .class files and provides a
   platform-independent execution environment.”

4. what is JRE?
   “JRE (Java Runtime Environment) provides the environment required to run Java applications. It includes the JVM and
   supporting libraries needed to execute Java bytecode.”2

5. what is JDK?
   Used to develop Java applications; includes development tools such as the compiler.
   “JDK (Java Development Kit) is a software development kit used to develop, compile, and run Java applications. It
   includes the JRE components, JVM, Java compiler (javac), and other development tools.”

6. what are checked Exceptions?
   “Checked exceptions are checked by the compiler at compile time, but they can occur during runtime when an operation
   fails. For example, FileNotFoundException can occur when we try to open a file that does not exist.”
   Ex:- FileNotFoundException

7. what is Fully checked Exception?
   An exception class is said to be fully checked if and only if all its child classes are also checked exceptions.”
   Ex:-IOException

8. what is UncheckedException?
   “Unchecked exceptions are exceptions that are not checked by the compiler at compile time and occur during runtime.”
   Ex;-
   NullPointerException
   ArithmeticException
   ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException

9. How do you handle the Exceptions?
   “We can handle exceptions in Java using try-catch blocks. We place the code that may cause an exception inside the
   try block and handle the exception in the corresponding catch block. We can use finally to execute cleanup code, and
   throw and throws to throw or declare exceptions.”
   Keyword  	 Purpose
   try	         Contains code that may throw an exception
   catch	     Handles an exception
   finally 	     Executes cleanup code, usually whether an exception occurs or not

10. what happen if the Exception is occur in the Catch block?
    If an exception occurs inside a catch block, that exception will not be handled by the same catch block. It propagates
    to the caller unless another suitable handler catches it.

11. method to print Exception information.
    printStackTrace()-- name of the Exception :description of the Exception :stackTrace
    toString()       -- Name of the Exception :Description of the Exception
    getMessage()     -- Description

12. what is final key word?
    In Java, final is a keyword that can be used at the variable, method, and class levels to restrict modifications.
    Variable level: Once assigned, a final variable cannot be reassigned.
    Method level: A final method cannot be overridden by a subclass.
    Class level: A final class cannot be extended or inherited.
13. what is finally?
    finally is a block in Java that is used with a try block to execute cleanup activities, such as closing database
    connections, file resources, and other resources. It generally executes whether an exception occurs or not.

14. what us finalize()?
    is a method always invoked by garbage collector just before destroying an object to perform cleanup activities.one's
    finalize() completes immediately GC destroy the object.

15. try with resources?
    Try-with-resources was introduced in Java 7. It automatically closes resources declared within the try statement when
    the block finishes execution, whether an exception occurs or not. The resources must implement the AutoCloseable or
    Closeable interface

16. what is throw keyword?
    throw is a keyword in Java used to explicitly throw an exception object from a method or block of code.
    public class Main {
        public static void main(String[] args) {
            int age = 15;

            if (age < 18) {
                throw new IllegalArgumentException("Not eligible");
            }
        }
    }

17. throws keyword?
    throws is a keyword in Java used in a method declaration to specify the exceptions that the method may throw. It
    indicates that the responsibility for handling or propagating those exceptions is passed to the caller.”
    --- we can use throws for methods and constructors but not for classes
    --- we can use throws for only throwable types
    --- we can use it for only Exceptions not for errors
    --- with in try block if there is a chance of rising an exception then we can't write catch block for that Exception.
        otherwise we will get compile time error saying :Exception is never thrown

18. How to create UserDefined Exceptions?
    We can create user-defined or custom exceptions by creating a class that extends RuntimeException for an unchecked
    exception or Exception for a checked exception. We typically define a constructor that accepts a String message and
    passes it to the parent class using super(message)

19. what is classCastException when it is occurred?
    ClassCastException is an unchecked exception that occurs at runtime when we try to cast an object to an incompatible
    class type

20. NoClassDefFoundError?
    “NoClassDefFoundError is an unchecked error that occurs when the JVM cannot find a class definition that was
    available when the code was compiled, typically because the class is missing from the runtime classpath or a
    required class failed to initialize.”

21. ClassNotFoundException?
    “ClassNotFoundException is a checked exception that occurs when an application tries to load a class dynamically
     by its name, but the class cannot be found by the class loader.”

22. can we rethrow an Exception?
    “Yes, we can rethrow an exception by catching it in a catch block and using the throw keyword to throw the same
    exception again. This allows the exception to propagate to the calling method for further handling.”
    public class Main {
        static void process() {
            try {
                int result = 10 / 0;
            } catch (ArithmeticException e) {
                System.out.println("Logging the exception");
                throw e; // Rethrowing the same exception
            }
        }

        public static void main(String[] args) {
            try {
                process();
            } catch (ArithmeticException e) {
                System.out.println("Exception handled by caller");
            }
        }
    }















